uestion button isn't present here. That means I can't generate a selector for it based on the current HTML. I'll have to wait until the user logs in and the dashboard HTML is provided before handling that interaction.\n\nSo, I'll include selectors for clicking the login button, typing the username, and typing the password. Each of these has a high confidence score because they're using ids, which are unique and reliable. The XPaths are also provided as alternatives, though they might be more brittle if the structure changes.\n\nI need to make sure each selector follows the rules: prefer IDs, avoid overly specific selectors, and provide reasoning. Each interaction is separate, so I'll create a SelectorSchema object for each action.\n</think>\n\n[\n  {\n    \"element\": \"Login button\",\n    \"selector\": \"#loginForm button[type=\\\"submit\\\"]\",\n    \"xpath\": \"/html/body/div[2]/form/button\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.95,\n    \"reasoning\": \"High confidence due to unique form ID and button type.\",\n    \"interaction_type\": \"click\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"element\": \"Username input field\",\n    \"selector\": \"#username\",\n    \"xpath\": \"/html/body/div[2]/form/div[1]/input\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.95,\n    \"reasoning\": \"High confidence due to unique ID.\",\n    \"interaction_type\": \"type\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"element\": \"Password input field\",\n    \"selector\": \"#password\",\n    \"xpath\": \"/html/body/div[2]/form/div[2]/input\",\n    \"confidence\": 0.95,\n    \"reasoning\": \"High confidence due to unique ID.\",\n    \"interaction_type\": \"type\"\n  }\n]"}
