 the correct IDs. So, I'll proceed with using #email and #password as the selectors.\n\nFor the submit button, the HTML shows a button with id \"submit\", so I can use #submit for that.\n\nNow, considering the rules, I should prefer ID selectors. So, for the username input, I'll use #email, and for the password, #password. The submit button is #submit.\n\nI also need to provide XPath alternatives. For the username input, the XPath would be xpath=//input[@id=\"email\"], and similarly for the password. For the submit button, it's xpath=//button[@id=\"submit\"].\n\nConfidence scores: Since we're using ID selectors, which are very specific, the confidence is high, around 0.9 for each.\n\nThe interaction types are \"type\" for the username and password, and \"click\" for the submit button.\n\nI should also include the text to type into the fields as per the user story: \"manish1217.be22@chitkarauniversity.edu.in\" for the username and \"MaNiShRaJpUt_9211\" for the password.\n\nSince the \"BOOKMARKS\" element isn't present in the current HTML, I can't include it in this response. It will have to be handled in the next step after the page loads the bookmarks section.\n\nSo, the selectors I'll return are for filling the username, filling the password, and clicking the submit button. Each with their respective selectors, XPaths, confidence, reasoning, interaction types, and text.\n\nI also need to set shouldContinue appropriately. Since after clicking submit, the page will load a new HTML, the last interaction (submit) should have shouldContinue set to false to stop the workflow until the next HTML is provided.\n\nWait, no. Actually, in the response, each selector is an interaction. So, the first interaction is typing the username, then the password, then clicking submit. Each should have shouldContinue set to true except the last one, which should be false to indicate the end of this set of interactions.\n\nBut looking at the example response, each object has shouldContinue